MEMORY CHECK: SYMPTOM-DRIVEN TRIAGE

SYMPTOM: cannot start program
  ps
  cat /proc/memory
  cat /proc/ramfiles
  ls /tmp
A user slot may be unavailable, or image/stack may exceed the
24-KiB slot. Do not assume a single error has one cause.

SYMPTOM: suspicious process or file growth
  ps
  lsof
  cat /proc/ramfiles
  hexdump /tmp/NAME 0 64
Read a bounded preview, then compare with expected file content.
/tmp RAM-file size limit and user-program slot limit are DIFFERENT.

SYMPTOM: changing memory bytes
  hexdump /dev/shm 0 32
  hexdump /dev/shm 0 32
If changes persist, inspect whether another process owns the shared
region. Do not claim a leak from two snapshots.

SYMPTOM: network allocation pressure
  cat /proc/net/memory
  ps
  lsof
Correlate network heap, pbuf and PCB counters with socket owners.
A counter snapshot cannot establish a leak; repeated controlled
measurements and a terminating test workload are needed.

STOP CONDITIONS
  Unknown address range, unknown live hardware ownership, output
  truncation, or repeated MCP transport failures: stop raw probing
  and report observations. Avoid full-screen mem and watch over MCP.
